# FEATURE-DOCTOR-TRUSTED-ANDROID-DEVICE-LOCK-1 Phase 3.5 — durable rules

Decision: `docs/adr/0009-android-production-signing-distribution-and-device-management.md`.
Config: `config/android_release.php`. Gate: `php artisan android:release-readiness --strict`.
Supersedes rule 11 of `140-android-clinic-device-identity.mdc` ("production
signing is UNRESOLVED"). Rules 1–10 and 12–14 of 140 are unchanged.

## 1. Enforcement is STILL off

Phase 3.5 changed no runtime behaviour. `DEVICE_ENFORCEMENT_ACTIVE=false`,
`DOCTOR_BROWSER_LOGIN_DENIED=false`, and **no feature flag exists**. Phase 2
decided the flag is created by the phase that needs one; creating it "in
preparation" is how a half-wired switch gets flipped by someone who assumes it
is finished. If you are adding one, you are starting Phase 5.

## 2. Signing authority — SUPERSEDED by rule 142

> **REVISION-DOCTOR-ANDROID-DIRECT-APK-SIGNING-DISTRIBUTION-1 replaced this.**
> Play App Signing is **NOT used**. DaengtisiaMS owns the production app signing
> key outright, and its loss is **unrecoverable** — see
> `.cursor/rules/142-android-direct-apk-signing-distribution.mdc` §2 and
> `docs/adr/0010-android-direct-apk-signing-and-distribution.md`.
>
> What survives from the original clause is its warning, which was accurate:
> holding the app signing key yourself means nobody can restore it. Rule 142
> answers that with three custodians, three encrypted copies and a 90-day
> restore drill.

## 3. Never create a production key to make a sprint green

A production signing identity is created only under recorded custody with named
custodian roles. An irreplaceable key generated on an ephemeral context that
nobody owns is worse than no key — Phase 3.5 shipped with **no** production key
rather than an unowned one. Rehearse procedures with a **disposable** key and
destroy it afterwards.

Never report a disposable-key drill as production-key verification. The honest
pair is `SIGNING_RECOVERY_RUNBOOK_TESTED_WITH_DISPOSABLE_KEY=true` /
`PRODUCTION_KEY_RECOVERY_VERIFIED=false`.

## 4. Never debug-sign a release

The debug key is a publicly known identity — anyone can sign an update over it.
The `release` build type must not reference debug signing. Gate:
`release_not_debug_signed`.

## 5. Key material never enters the repository or PR CI

Checked against the **git index**, not the working tree: an ignored file on disk
says nothing about history. Pull-request CI executes untrusted code, so a key it
can reach makes every contributor a release authority. `android:release-readiness`
is deliberately safe on a fork PR because it holds nothing.

## 6. versionCode is monotonic, and rollback is forward

Play refuses a versionCode that is not strictly greater, and Android blocks the
downgrade install. "Reinstall the previous APK" is not an available operation.
Recovery = **stop distribution**, then ship the older code under a **higher**
versionCode. ("Halt the rollout" was a Play Console button; see rule 142 §8.)
Devices that already updated stay on the bad version until a fix reaches them.

Rule 142 §7 adds a precision this clause hid: the **platform** rule is `>=`,
and only Play enforced strictly `>`. That stricter half is now ours to enforce.

## 7. Distribution — SUPERSEDED by rule 142

> **Managed Google Play is NOT used.** Distribution is a signed **APK**
> installed directly by authorised DaengtisiaMS Admin/IT; the artifact is an
> APK, not an App Bundle; and the Phase-3.5 "pilot exception" is **deleted**
> rather than widened, because with direct installation canonical the pilot and
> the fleet use the same path. See rule 142 §1, §4 and §5.
>
> The clause below that still holds: the package name is permanent from first
> install — now for a platform reason (package + certificate are the update
> identity) rather than a Play one.

## 8. Kiosk is Device Owner, and at scale the DPC is the owner

Two supported models: self-owned Device Owner (pilot) and EMM dedicated device
(fleet, ≥5 devices). Under the EMM model **our app is not the Device Owner** —
the DPC is, and it lock-task-allowlists our package.

Forbidden permanently: screen pinning (user-dismissible), launcher replacement
alone, hidden exit gestures, hard-coded exit PINs. QR provisioning payloads must
never carry secrets.

## 9. Real-device validation is a Phase 4A / 4B blocker

`ANDROID_REAL_DEVICE_VALIDATION=false` until a clinic tablet is validated
against the acceptance checklist. Hardware-backed and StrongBox results are
**recorded truthfully**, whatever the device reports — never assumed, never
rounded up. Emulator results are never described as real-device validation.

Refined by the Phase 4A/4B split (§16): the **app-only** half of that checklist
belongs to Phase 4A and the **Device Owner / kiosk** half to Phase 4B. Reporting
either half as the whole is the failure this rule guards against. Device
*capability* reported by `getprop` or `pm list features` is not per-key
`KeyInfo` attestation, and the two are never conflated.

## 10. Device spec is anchored to Android Enterprise Recommended

AER is the authority because it binds an OEM to a published security-update end
date. The AER directory is checked **at purchase time** — devices are archived
from it when an OEM stops shipping updates, so a model list frozen into config
becomes a procurement trap. Dated candidates live in the procurement doc; config
holds criteria only.

## 11. Enforcement never precedes spares

Before global enforcement: pilot passed, **every** enforced Doctor has an ACTIVE
device, a spare per branch, the device-loss runbook rehearsed, and rollback to
browser login proven. Browser login is the fallback — removing that door before
spares exist is how a clinic loses a day.

## 12. Lost or stolen device: REVOKE, immediately

Revocation is terminal by design. An administrator who is unsure should revoke:
being wrong costs five minutes of re-enrolment, and the other error is a trusted
device in someone else's hands. Never delete a device row — it is the security
history.

## 13. A server deploy must not brick an un-updated tablet

No minimum-client-version hard block is implemented, deliberately. Breaking
device API changes take a **new path**; the old one survives the grace period.
Ship the tolerant server first, then the client, then retire the old path.

## 14. Source-scanning guards: comment-aware, literal-**aware**, never vacuous

Rule 13 of 140 says strip comments so a guard stops matching the prose
explaining it. Do not over-apply it: the forbidden token here is
`signingConfigs.getByName("debug")`, which **contains a string literal**. Blank
the strings and the token becomes unmatchable and the guard passes on a
genuinely debug-signed release. Literal awareness means "a `//` inside a string
does not open a comment" — not "delete the string".

Keep a presence assertion beside every absence assertion, so a stripper that
removes too much fails loudly instead of passing on nothing. Kotlin block
comments nest.

## 15. Forbidden literals live in config, never in the scanner

A guard whose own source contains the string it forbids is a guard that fails on
itself. Every pattern is declared in `config/android_release.php`.

## 16. Phase 4A is app-only; Phase 4B is Device Owner — and neither softens §3

The original Phase 4 was all-or-nothing because it required Device Owner, which
on a device that has completed setup or holds any account demands a **factory
reset**. That put a personal, in-use tablet on the critical path and blocked the
whole phase. The phase is therefore split:

- **Phase 4A — app-only real-device pilot.** Production-signed APK, real Android
  Keystore identity, cryptographic enrolment, approval, pilot-scoped enforcement,
  session/device binding, branch lock, room scope, print denial. An **existing
  personal tablet is acceptable** here. `DEVICE_OWNER=false` and `KIOSK=false`
  are declared states, not gaps.
- **Phase 4B — dedicated-device hardening.** Device Owner, Lock Task, escape
  blocking, reboot behaviour. Requires a **dedicated, factory-fresh** tablet.

Never factory-reset, wipe, remove accounts from, or set Device Owner on a
personal or shared device to unblock a phase. If a step requires destructive
provisioning it is Phase 4B work by definition, and stops.

**Phase 4A does not weaken §3 or §4.** An app-only pilot is still a pilot: it
needs the real production signing key under recorded custody, and installing a
debug-signed artifact on a pilot device is forbidden because it validates a
signing identity production will never use. An install proven against the wrong
trust anchor is worse than no proof — it reads as evidence. No key → no signed
APK → no pin → no verified install → no enrolment; the chain is arithmetic, and
no amount of tooling substitutes for the custody decision at its head.

## 17. The pilot enforcement stage already exists — do not build a second one

`enforcement.stages` already runs `off` → `pilot_branch_or_device` →
`selected_doctors_approved_device_set` → `all_ready_doctors` →
`global_doctor_enforcement`, and `global_prerequisites` already scopes the
real-device-pilot and spare-device conditions to **global** enforcement
specifically. Pilot enforcement is a governed advance along that ladder, not a
new abstraction, and a sprint that invents a parallel `OFF/PILOT/GLOBAL` model
is duplicating shipped governance.

Editing a phase bound is a **sign-off decision, never a code change**, and it
moves only with `requires_owner_signoff` satisfied and a rollback owner named
before start. The pilot Doctor, pilot branch and rollback owner are supplied
explicitly by the owner and are **never guessed, never defaulted to the first
Doctor found**.

REVISION-ANDROID-RELEASE-READINESS-PHASE4A-PILOT-AUTHORITY-1 split the bound,
because one number could not carry two answers. A sign-off that reads "Phase 4
is approved" slides into "enforcement is approved", and the rung that locks
every Doctor out of a browser is one line further down the same ladder:

- `enforcement.pilot_may_be_enabled_in_phase` — `'4A'`. The
  `pilot_branch_or_device` rung, one named Doctor at one named branch.
- `enforcement.global_may_be_enabled_in_phase` — `5`. The
  `global_doctor_enforcement` rung. **Never earlier, whatever a pilot sign-off
  says.**
- `enforcement.may_be_enabled_in_phase` — **retained** because this rule, ADR
  0009/0011 and both Phase-4 closure records cite it by name. It is the GLOBAL
  bound, it declares that with `may_be_enabled_in_phase_scope`, and the scanner
  FAILs if it ever drifts from `global_may_be_enabled_in_phase`. It does **not**
  mean "nothing may be enabled before Phase 5".

`enforcement.global_enforcement_stage` names the global rung rather than
deriving it from the last array element, so appending a future rung cannot
silently move what "global" means.
